012350553879514337], [" better", -7.612174034118652]]}, {"t": " targeted", "lp": -0.015050329267978668, "top": [[" targeted", -0.015050329267978668], [" tailored", -4.47995138168335]]}, {"t": " advice", "lp": -0.19926445186138153, "top": [[" advice", -0.19926445186138153], [" guidance", -1.7139129638671875]]}, {"t": " on", "lp": -0.09889037162065506, "top": [[" on", -0.09889037162065506], [" about", -2.5634593963623047]]}, {"t": "?", "lp": -0.0001791279937606305, "top": [["?", -0.0001791279937606305], ["\uff1f", -9.992637634277344]]}, {"t": "", "lp": -0.0044786338694393635, "top": [["", -0.0044786338694393635], ["\n\n", -6.056878089904785]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are handled by the Xe driver. Here's what each mode does:\n\n## CCS Mode 1\n- **Context stealing / preemption-based**\n- CCS contexts are allocated dynamically from the available CCS instances\n- The driver manages context migration between GCS and CCS\n- More flexible but can have overhead from context management\n\n## CCS Mode 2\n- **Static/dedicated CCS assignment**\n- CCS contexts are pinned to specific CCS instances\n- More predictable behavior since contexts don't migrate\n- Lower overhead but less flexible allocation\n\n## CCS Mode 4\n- Typically used for **specific debugging or testing scenarios**\n- Often relates to forcing particular CCS behavior for diagnostics\n- Less commonly needed for general use\n\n---\n\n**Important caveat:** I want to be honest with you that I'm not fully certain about the exact semantics of these specific mode numbers, as the `ccs_mode` parameter is a fairly low-level driver knob and the precise definitions of modes 1, 2, and 4 have shifted across driver versions.\n\n**What I'd recommend:**\n1. Check your actual driver source or docs:\n   ```bash\n   modinfo xe | grep ccs_mode\n   cat /sys/module/xe/parameters/ccs_mode\n   ```\n2. Look at the kernel documentation for the Xe driver\n3. Check the `xe` driver source in your kernel tree for the parameter definition\n\nWould you like me to help you interpret the actual output from your system, or are you working with a specific use case (like compute workloads, gaming, or debugging) that I could give more targeted advice on?"}